your MP Search for Members by name, postcode, or constituency†members.parliament.uk】 【17†Search for Members of the House of Lords Learn about their experience, knowledge and interests.†members.parliament.uk】\nL64: \nL65: ## About Parliament\nL66: \nL67: Parliament examines what the Government is doing, makes new laws, holds the power to set taxes and debates the issues of the day.\nL68: \nL69: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL70: ### In this section\nL71: \nL72:   * 【23†About Parliament home】\nL73:   * 【24†How Parliament works】\nL74:   * 【25†About MPs, Lords & officers】\nL75:   * 【26†Independent Complaints and Grievance Scheme (ICGS)】\nL76:   * 【27†Living Heritage】\nL77:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL78:   * 【29†Frequently Asked Questions】\nL79:   * 【30†Sustainability and environmental performance in Parliament】\nL80:   * 【31†UK Parliament's Modern Slavery Programme】\nL81: ## Get involved\nL82: \nL83: Read about how to contact an MP or Lord, petition Parliament and find out details of events in your area.\nL84: \nL85: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL86: \nL87: ### In this section\nL88: \nL89:   * 【32†Get involved home】\nL90:   * 【33†Contact an MP or a Member of the Lords】\nL91:   * 【34†Join a learning programme】\nL92:   * 【35†Research impact at the UK Parliament】\nL93:   * 【36†Have your say: Laws and debates】\nL94:   * 【37†Y Gornel Gymraeg】\nL95: ### Get involved with Parliament\nL96: \nL97: 【33†Contact an MP or Lord Contact your MP or a Member of the House of Lords about an issue that matters to you.】 【38†Keep up to date Sign up for the Your Parliament newsletter to find out how you can get involved.†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL98: \nL99: ### It starts with action\nL100: \nL101:   * 【39†Sign or create a petition】\nL102:   * 【40†Sign up to UK Parliament Week†www.ukparliamentweek.org】\nL103: ## Visit Parliament\nL104: \nL105: Find out how to take a tour in person, join an online event, book tickets and plan a visit to UK Parliament.\nL106: \nL107: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL108: \nL109: ### In this section\nL110: \nL111:   * 【41†Visit Parliament home】\nL112:   * 【42†Tours】\nL113:   * 【43†Talks and events】\nL114:   * 【44†Watch debates and committees】\nL115:   * 【45†Plan your visit】\nL116:   * 【46†360° virtual tour】\nL117:   * 【47†Venue hire】\nL118:   * 【3†Parliament Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL119: ### Keep up to date\nL120: \nL121: 【48†Visit newsletter Sign up to our newsletter to receive the latest news about visiting UK Parliament.†parliament.us14.list-manage.com】\nL122: \nL123: ## Learning\nL124: \nL125: Your UK Parliament offers free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators to spark engagement and active citizenship.\nL126: \nL127: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL128: ### In this section\nL129: \nL130:   * 【49†Learning home†learning.parliament.uk】\nL131:   * 【50†About UK Parliament education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # Voting systems in the UK\nL156: \nL157: Voting systems, or electoral systems, are the method by which we elect representatives.  A voting system determines the rules on how we elect parties and candidates.\nL158: \nL159: The House of Commons, Scottish Parliament, Welsh Parliament, Northern Ireland Assembly and UK local authorities use different voting systems.\nL160: ## First-past-the-post\nL161: \nL162: The House of Commons, mayoral elections in England, Police and Crime Commissioner elections and local councils in England and Wales use the first-past-the-post system.\nL163: \nL164: Voters select their preferred candidate. The candidate with the most votes wins.\nL165: ## Alternative Vote (AV)\nL166: \nL167: Alternative Vote is used to elect:\nL168: \nL169:   * chairs of most 【58†committees】 in the House of Commons\nL170:   * the Lord Speaker and by-elections for 【59†hereditary peers】.\nL171: \nL172: Voters rank candidates in order of preference by marking 1, 2, 3 and so on. A voter can rank as many or as few candidates as they like or vote for one candidate.\nL173: \nL174: First preference votes are counted first. If a candidate receives more than 50 per cent of the first preference votes then they are elected.\nL175: If no candidate reaches 50 per cent, the candidate with the fewest first preference votes is eliminated. Their second preference votes are reallocated to the remaining candidates. If one candidate has more votes than the other remaining candidates put together, that candidate is elected.\nL176: \nL177: If not, the process is repeated until one candidate has more votes than the other remaining candidates put together. This candidate wins the election.\nL178: ## Supplementary Vote (SV)\nL179: \nL180: The SV system is like the AV system. Voters are limited to a first and second preference choice. A voter marks a cross in one column for their first preference candidate. They mark another cross in a second column for their second preference if they wish to do so.\nL181: \nL182: If a candidate receives more than 50 per cent of the first preference votes then they are elected.\nL183: If no candidate reaches the 50 per cent threshold, the two candidates with the highest number of votes remain. This eliminates the other candidates. The second preferences of voters who voted for eliminated candidates are then counted. Any made for the two remaining candidates are transferred. The candidate with the most votes at the end of this process is elected.\nL184: \nL185: Up until May 2023, elections for mayors in England and for Police and Crime Commissioners in England and Wales, used SV.\nL186: ## Single Transferable Vote (STV)\nL187: \nL188: Single Transferable Vote is used for:\nL189: \nL190:   * Elections for 【60†Deputy Speakers】 in the House of Commons\nL191:   * Northern Ireland Assembly elections\nL192:   * Local elections in Scotland and Northern Ireland\nL193: \nL194: STV was also used in Northern Ireland to elect Members of the European Parliament from 1979 to 2019.\nL195: \nL196: Constituencies which elect more than one representative use STV.\nL197: Voters rank candidates in order of preference by marking 1, 2, 3 and so on. A voter can rank as many or as few candidates as they like or vote for only one candidate.\nL198: \nL199: Each candidate needs to reach a quota. This is the minimum number of votes calculated according to the number of seats and votes cast.\nL200: \nL201: The first preference votes for each candidate are added up. Candidates who achieve this quota are elected.\nL202: \nL203: Surplus votes from candidates who hit the quota go to second preference candidates.\nL204: The candidate with the fewest first preference votes who do not achieve the quota are eliminated. Voters who voted for an eliminated candidate then have their votes transferred to their second preference. The process of transferring surpluses and second (or third, fourth, and so on) preferences continues until all vacant seats have been filled.\nL205: ## Additional Member System (AMS)\nL206: \nL207: The Additional Member System is used by:\nL208: \nL209:   * the Scottish Parliament\nL210:   * the Welsh Parliament\nL211:   * the London Assembly\nL212: \nL213: Voters are given two votes on separate ballot papers. One vote is for a constituency member and one vote is for a party list. In Scotland and Wales list members are elected by region. In London there is a single London-wide list.\nL214: \nL215: Constituency votes are counted first and the members for each constituency are elected using first-past-the-post.\nL216: Additional members are then elected by counting the party list votes in each region. The number of members elected from the list is based on the percentage of the votes cast but also takes into account the number of constituency members already elected in the region. This is designed to make the result more proportional to the number of votes cast.\nL217: ## Closed Party List\nL218: \nL219: The Closed Party List system was used in England, Scotland and Wales to elect Members of the European Parliament between 1999 and 2019.\nL220: \nL221: Under this system, a voter marks a cross on the ballot paper next to the party's name. Parties get the number of seats in proportion to the votes it receives in each constituency.\nL222: \nL223: Voters choose parties not candidates. The parties determine the order in which candidates appear on the list.\nL224: ## See also\nL225: \nL226:   * 【61†Elections and voting】\nL227:   * 【62†Devolved Parliaments and Assemblies】\nL228: \nL229: ## Related information\nL230: \nL231: ### Parliament and constitution research\nL232: \nL233: Read House of Commons Library briefing papers to find out more information about 【63†voting systems in the UK†researchbriefings.parliament.uk】.\nL234: ### Second ballot\nL235: \nL236: Under the Second Ballot system a first-past-the-post election is held. If no candidate gets more than 50 per cent of the vote, the candidates who fail to reach a specified threshold are eliminated.\nL237: \nL238: A second election is held (usually several days later) and the candidate who receives the most votes in this second ballot is elected.\nL239: ### Limited vote\nL240: \nL241: Limited Vote systems use multi-member constituencies.  Voters, however, have fewer votes than there are available seats.\nL242: \nL243: An electoral system used in multi-member constituencies in which electors have more than one vote, but fewer votes than there are candidates to be elected. The candidates with the most votes are elected.\nL244: ###### Popular\nL245: \nL246:   * 【64†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL247:   * 【65†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL248:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL249:   * 【66†Commons Media Relations】\nL250:   * 【67†Lords Press Office】\nL251:   * 【68†Contact us】\nL252: \nL253: ###### Research\nL254: \nL255:   * 【69†Commons Library†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL256:   * 【70†Lords Library†lordslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL257:   * 【71†POST†post.parliament.uk】\nL258: \nL259: ###### Search Parliament\nL260: \nL261: [Input]\nL262: \nL263: [Input]\nL264: \nL265: ###### Watch Parliament Live\nL266: \nL267: 【72†Parliament Live TV†parliamentlive.tv】\nL268: ###### Follow Parliament\nL269: \nL270: ###### Legal\nL271: \nL272:   * 【73†Accessibility statement】\nL273:   * 【31†Modern slavery statement】\nL274:   * 【74†About this site】\nL275:   * 【75†Privacy notice】\nL276:   * 【76†Cookie policy】\nL277:   * 【2†Cookie settings】\nL278: \nL279: © UK Parliament 2026\n"
